[Result "*"]
[FEN "4B3/2K5/3P4/2k5/8/7b/8/8 w - - 0 1"]

 { This is what we could name the Second Case: the defending king has reached rear opposition. From his position he hinders an essential manoeuvre: Now the stronger side cannot offer a bishop trade without obstructing the pawn ! The driving-off manoeuvre does not work now: } 1. Bd7 { This is the only square available for the bishop exchange: on c8 White would lose the pawn. The black bishop moves away but gets ready to occupy the other diagonal (a4-e8). } Bf1 2. Bg4 Bb5 { It becomes apparent now that, if the black king did not control c6, White would have the winning manoeuvre Bf3-c6, but now it is impossible. } 3. Bd7 Be2 4. Bc6 Bg4 $11 { It is an ironclad draw. Conclusion: When the defending king cannot stand in front of the pawn, the ideal position for him is behind the stronger side's king, in opposition.As we will see right now, we should not rush to assume that this defensive technique always brings a draw. } 
