 { If we apply the previous rules, this position is quite easy to understand. Where should the white king go ? It is logical: where he can control more squares of the short diagonal. } 1. Ke8 $2 { Let us look at the wrong move and make sure that is impossible to win that way. } ( 1. Kg8 $1 Kg6 2. Bf8 { wins in the same fashion as in the previous example. } ) 
Ke6 ( { Adopting rear opposition, an easy and known method, from the beginning. We have plenty of time, so here we could play first } 1... Kg6 ) 
 ( { or } 1... Kf5 { . However, at the key moment, when the white bishop goes to e7, the black king should always be ready to get to e6. } ) 
2. Bf8 Bf4 3. Bb4 Bh6 4. Bd2 Bg7 { The bishop has one more square. } 5. Be3 { Semi-zugzwang. Black has to move the king, but nothing happens. } Kd6 6. Bd2 Ke6 { Time to go back. It is interesting to note that other king moves would lose, since they would allow the white king to make it to the other side of the pawn. } ( 6... Kd5 7. Ke7 Kc6 ( { or } 7... Be5 8. Kf8 $1 ) 
8. Ke6 { and, with the king on the other side of the pawn, White wins. The winning sequence is easy to analyse and I suggest doing it as an Exercise. } ) 
7. Bc3 Bh6 { Draw. There is no way to make progress. The white king controls just one square on the short diagonal, so it is impossible to win. } 
