 { There are not many studies about this situation, but I have found some mistakes in its application in practice. We can consider this the Fourth Case. Kurajica - Markland, Hastings 1971/72 Since we already know the basic situations, let us start with this position, still far from the key moment: } 1... Kd7 $6 { The black player shows his ignorance of the basic drawing procedure, thus making life difficult for himself. The simplest defensive plan was } ( 1... Kd5 $1 2. Kg6 Ke4 3. Bc7 Kf3 4. Bd8 Kg4 $11 { and the black king makes it to the rear of the pawn, getting the opposition. } ) 
2. Kg6 Ke8 $1 { Now there was no time to get to the g4-square, but it is still a draw. Black can try another drawing procedure. It is less well known and more delicate, but worth knowing, in case one reaches a position where the rear opposition defence is not available. The king heads for the g8-square. } 3. Bf4 { Trying to cut off the black king’s path to f8 would not work. Black just waits: } ( 3. Bd6 Bd4 $11 { . } ) 
Bd4 $2 { However, Black fails to find the second defensive method as well. The correct defense was } ( 3... Kf8 $1 4. Bh6 Kg8 $1 { The key point is that when the black king is here, White cannot offer the bishop exchange on g5, since it leads to a drawn pawn endgame. } 5. Bg5 Bd4 6. Bf6 { . As usual, if the attacking side offers a bishop exchange in front of the pawn, the defending bishop just switches to another diagonal. } Bf2 7. Be5 Bh4 8. Bf4 Bd8 9. Bg5 { . This confirms the draw but, anyway, there is no way to make progress: } Bxg5 10. Kxg5 Kf7 $11 { . } ) 
4. Bh6 $1 ( { And Black resigned, maybe because the game was adjourned here. In any case, victory comes easily, since White can offer the bishop exchange without obstructing his pawn: } 4. Bh6 Ke7 5. Bg7 Bc5 6. f6 Bc3 5. Bg7 Bb4 6. f6 $18 { . Conclusion: Frontal opposition when the defending king is next to the promotion square works if the pawn cannot go further than the 6th rank. } ) 

