 { Averbakh, 1972 An interesting position. We can revise some of the themes previously introduced and note some well known tricks. } 1. Bh5 $5 { The only attempt to win. If White allows 1... Ke8, the black king reaches d8, with an immediate draw. } Bh3 { There are two possible winning attempts. Black has to be careful in either case. } 2. Ke5 $5 { The most dangerous winning attempt. Black can draw if he grasps White's plan and knows the defensive technique of transferring the king to the rear of the pawn. The white plan is to take his king to the c7-square. Black's only defence involves transferring his king to c5. There is time enough for this, but Black will not do it unless he knows what is going on. } ( 2. Bg6 { White tries to win by trading bishops on f5. It looks like a terrible threat, but it is easy to prevent: } Bd7 $1 { Only move. Note that the position would be more dangerous with a knight's pawn. Then a zugzwang would arise, because the bishop could not go to the short diagonal. Now the bishop can move along the other diagonal. } ( 2. Ke5 Bg4 $2 3. Bf5 $1 { winning the pawn endgame } Bxf5 4. Kxf5 Kf7 5. Ke5 Kf8 6. Kf6 ) 
3. Bf5 { White burns his bridges. } Ke8 $11 { Now the king reaches a black square, and it is a clear draw. } Bc6 { is not losing either, but it is important to point out that, with a knight’s pawn, the corresponding move i.e. ...Ba6 would lose. You can check it as an interesting Exercise. } ) 
Bd7 $6 ( { Waiting means just losing, though this move does not bring immediate disaster. } 2... Kg7 $1 { If we know Endings 33 and 34, we can understand this is the right defensive procedure for Black. } ) 
3. Kd5 Ba4 $2 { It was not too late for the king to arrive: } ( 3... Kg7 $1 { - the only move now - } 4. Kc5 Kf6 5. Kb6 Ke5 6. Kc7 Bh3 ( 6... Ba4 { would lose a tempo and the game: } 7. Bf3 $1 Kd4 8. Bc6 $18 ) 
7. Be8 Kd4 $1 8. Bd7 Bf1 9. Bg4 Bb5 10. Bf3 Kc5 $11 { Just in time ! } ) 
4. Kc5 Bd7 5. Kb6 Ba4 6. Kc7 { See Ending 33. } 
